    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense, but my students always struggle to visualize how those two things actually happen at the same time. It feels like they're two separate concepts rather than one fluid process. Is there a way to frame the interaction between the pressure difference and the air deflection more intuitively? Like, maybe a way to describe the actual physical \"action\" occurring at the wing surface?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Imagine the wing as both a pressure differential machine and a redirector simultaneously. The air rushing over the curved top accelerates, creating the low-pressure \"suction\" force, while the downward deflection of air underneath pushes against the wing surface with a reactive force. You can visualize it as the low pressure *pulling* the wing up, while the deflected air *shoves* the wing up, with both forces adding to the total lift. This dual action is what makes the airfoil so effective.",
      "source": "target",
